// title: Output refs: what they are, and are not
// desc: A failure mints a handle to that run's exact bytes - not a cache key, not deterministic, and not portable off your machine.
// doc: concepts/output-refs|Output references
//
// Output references: what they are, and what they are not. Run `run build` and
// this target fails on purpose. Under the failure card magus prints a short id:
//
//   [fail] demo build (ran, 4ms)
//     cause: exit status 1
//     output: out1a2b3c4d
//     inspect: magus query output out1a2b3c4d
//
// That `out...` id is an OUTPUT REFERENCE: a handle to the bytes ONE execution
// wrote. `magus query output <ref>` replays them verbatim - never a
// reconstruction, never a summary - so the failing log survives the scrollback
// even after a hundred later runs.
//
// What it is NOT is the more useful half:
//
//   Not your cache key. The cache key is the SHA-256 of a target's inputs and
//   decides whether work runs at all. A ref identifies an EXECUTION, and the
//   same key can have many. They answer different questions: "has this been
//   built?" versus "what did that particular run print?".
//
//   Not deterministic, on purpose. A ref is minted fresh per execution rather
//   than derived from the key, because deriving it would collapse every run of
//   an unchanged target onto one id - and the whole reason you want a volatile
//   target's history is that its runs DIFFER. Two failures deserve two refs.
//
//   Not portable. A ref addresses your machine's local cache. Paste one from a
//   teammate's terminal, or from a CI log, and `query output` finds nothing:
//   the runner that minted it was deleted when the job ended. That is why magus
//   stops printing the `inspect:` hint when it detects CI - a command that
//   cannot work where it is read is worse than no hint at all.
//
//   Not an archive. magus keeps the last few executions per cache key and prunes
//   the rest. A ref is for the debugging loop you are in right now; anything you
//   need to keep, keep as an artifact.
//
// The short version: a cache key answers "did this change?", a ref answers "what
// happened that time?". Reach for `magus tail` to follow a run live, and a ref
// when you want the exact bytes of one that already finished.
//
// (The body below uses proc.exec, which runs a real command. magus.cmd is the escape
// hatch for invoking magus ITSELF - `magus\cmd("sh", ...)` would run `magus sh`.)

import "magus";
import "os";

import "proc";
magus\project({});

export fun build(ctx: magus\Context, args: [str]) > void {
    proc\exec("sh", ["-c", "echo 'compiling widget...'; echo 'error: undefined symbol' >&2; exit 1"], ".", {});
}
